## 4.5.0 (2026-09-08)

The glossary gets a way in and a way out, the accent colour is yours to pick,
and the local server stops answering web pages. 4.3.0 built no installers —
see the end.

### New:

-   **Add a glossary term without leaving the translation.** A bad rendering is the moment you want a term, and until now that meant going to Settings, typing both halves from memory, and coming back. The result panel has a button that prefills from what you have selected, scopes the entry to the pair actually being translated, and re-translates when you save it
-   **Import and export the glossary**, as CSV or JSON. The history page has had this all along; the glossary, which is the thing you actually build up by hand, had not
-   **Re-translate a row from history** — useful the moment you spot a bad OCR read, since it sends the text as you have edited it in the box
-   **Pick an accent colour.** One choice; the hover, the text on top of it, the focus ring and the link colour are worked out from it, and it sits on top of either theme. "Follow the theme" hands all of it back

### Security:

-   **The local server no longer answers the browser.** It listens on 127.0.0.1, which keeps the network out but not the page you are reading: a browser may send cross-origin requests to localhost, and while it cannot read the reply, the side effect still happened. Two of these endpoints take a screenshot — so a page could have had your screen captured and pushed through whichever OCR service you had configured, just by loading an image. It now takes POST only, and refuses anything a browser labels as cross-site. PopClip, SnipDo and any script of your own that POSTs are unaffected
-   **The server can be turned off**, under Settings → General, for anyone not using those extensions

### Bugs fixed:

-   the Recognize window's translate button made an HTTP round-trip to Gloss's own local server to reach a function in the same process; it would have broken for anyone turning that server off, and did break under the change above

### Note on 4.3.0:

That release built no macOS, Linux or WebView2-bundled Windows installers: the
rename to Gloss never reached the packaging workflow, which still looked for
files called `pot`, and the Linux image was missing a package the AppImage
bundler needs. Fixed here. Three jobs that published this fork's builds into
upstream pot's Homebrew, WinGet and docs channels have also been removed.
